  medical treatment for free but there's  no doctor that can help with the cold  that enters the makeshift coverings on  the glass 'less windows and there's no  prescription for homesickness I really  miss my home especially my neighbours in  Aleppo and my apartment I have no idea  what condition it's in a safe here in  Turkey but no country is as beautiful as  Syria there are some 300 thousand  refugees in Istanbul from Iraq as well  as Syria those who suffer social  rejection and are unable to find work  usually want to continue west to Europe  but for that they need people smugglers  to get them across the border to Greece  or to Italy we call the Syrian smuggler  in the Turkish port city of Marcin  pretending to be potential clients it  costs four thousand eight hundred  dollars per person usually we take  between five thousand and five thousand  five hundred the ship's seventy seven  meters long a freighter that can sail in  any kind of weather this time there will  be 1,000 passengers on board the captain  will not leave the ship don't worry  he'll only get his money when all the  refugees have reached their destination  the Mediterranean port of mersin some  1,000 kilometers south of Istanbul is a  hub for people smugglers  in the past desperate refugees used to  risk crossing to Europe in fishing boats  now they do it in freighters ready to be  scrapped this former journalist from  Damascus who wishes to remain anonymous  wants to use a smuggler to get to Europe  he no longer has a passport his wife and  children are in Lebanon now he's trying  to earn enough money for the crossing  despite the risks if you're trying to  escape death you don't care how  dangerous it is and Europe is forcing me  to place my fate in the hands of  smugglers there's no legal way to do it  I'll probably go alone and then get my  family when I've been granted asylum in  Italy or someplace else  these are Betar plans to stay in  Istanbul he says Arabs and Turks have a  lot in common in terms of culture and  religion and he feels at home in Turkey  you look for country respect you see you  give you peace give you life give you  war give you a warm give you medical  this is our country this is our hope I  must say that but only a very few of the  1.6 million Syrian refugees in Turkey  are as lucky as Nizar Betar or have his  entrepreneurial spirit
 

